WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.127
CTEs are mainly a query-organization tool.

00:00:03.127 --> 00:00:08.702
PostgreSQL can sometimes inline or fold a non-recursive CTE into the surrounding query.

00:00:08.702 --> 00:00:12.452
In other situations, materialization behavior can matter.

00:00:12.452 --> 00:00:16.822
The practical lesson for this course is simple: use CTEs for clarity,

00:00:16.822 --> 00:00:20.002
and inspect the execution plan when performance matters.

00:00:20.002 --> 00:00:23.267
Do not assume WITH automatically makes a query faster.

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:28.577
A CTE gives a name to an intermediate result so a complex query can be read in stages.

00:00:28.577 --> 00:00:35.602
Use it to clarify grain changes and major transformations, not simply to break every two lines into a new

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:41.625
block. A useful CTE name describes what the rows represent, such as patient_totals or ranked_visits.
